406
00:32:46,480 --> 00:32:47,967
Speaker 1: Alright, so anti-cheat.

407
00:32:48,028 --> 00:32:49,254
Speaker 1: because we just talked about how.

408
00:32:49,354 --> 00:32:54,779
Speaker 1: one of the reasons why I'm not willing to play Helldivers 2 right now is that there's onerous anti-cheat in a game that doesn't need it.

409
00:32:54,920 --> 00:32:57,449
Speaker 0: Yep, we've talked about cheating a lot.

410
00:32:57,650 --> 00:33:02,039
Speaker 0: We haven't actually talked about how to cheat or how to get away with cheating, have we?

411
00:33:03,280 --> 00:33:07,220
Speaker 0: We've talked about what is cheating and things of that nature.

412
00:33:08,463 --> 00:33:10,560
Speaker 0: But yeah, how do you actually prevent cheating?

413
00:33:10,800 --> 00:33:26,100
Speaker 0: If you are in charge of making, designing a game, or administering a game, or refereeing a game of some kind, what techniques and methods are effective in preventing cheating?

414
00:33:26,400 --> 00:33:37,516
Speaker 0: Because while all of us here won't actually cheat, there are other people in the world who are of ill moral repute and they will cheat.

415
00:33:37,937 --> 00:33:38,599
Speaker 0: They will do this.

416
00:33:39,582 --> 00:33:43,480
Speaker 0: And if they are playing your game, they are going to cheat at your game.

417
00:33:43,580 --> 00:33:46,520
Speaker 0: How can you detect them and stop them?

418
00:33:46,720 --> 00:33:54,045
Speaker 1: And I want to point out, it takes the exact same ingenuity to figure out how to catch a cheater as it does to cheat.

419
00:33:54,406 --> 00:33:59,619
Speaker 1: So people who are good at one are good at the other whether or not they use that power for good or evil.

420
00:34:00,400 --> 00:34:17,679
Speaker 0: Yep, now the very first thing is that regardless of the type of cheating and the type of game and the type of anything, in order to prevent or take any measure to stop cheating, the first thing you must always have is some kind of authority and power.

421
00:34:18,340 --> 00:34:23,795
Speaker 0: If you do not have authority and power of some kind within the game's realm, then you are hopeless.

422
00:34:25,321 --> 00:34:29,274
Speaker 0: There's nothing that you can do to prevent or stop cheating.

423
00:34:31,719 --> 00:34:40,620
Speaker 0: Now that power doesn't necessarily mean that you have to be like the judge at the Magic Tournament who actually has some power to disqualify someone.

424
00:34:40,659 --> 00:34:43,620
Speaker 1: But that power exists and is vested somewhere.

425
00:34:44,560 --> 00:34:44,741
Speaker 0: Right.

426
00:34:45,924 --> 00:35:00,859
Speaker 0: But that power simply might be, you called someone out, you have evidence, and the other people, you know, there are enough other people there who agree that that is not cool and are willing to rationally examine your evidence, etc.

427
00:35:01,442 --> 00:35:10,480
Speaker 0: It's like, you know, if you're just at a table with four friends, you're all equals playing the same game, and, you know, you might see that someone's just fucking cheating.

428
00:35:10,881 --> 00:35:13,720
Speaker 0: Your fucking uncle, he's just cheating over there, right?

429
00:35:14,021 --> 00:35:24,506
Speaker 1: We had this happen, remember that party years ago with a certain group of people, and they were playing one of those poker games where like one of your cards is visible on your forehead, and then we played another game with them.

430
00:35:24,526 --> 00:35:27,720
Speaker 1: that was some trivia game, it wasn't Pictionary, it was one of those games.

431
00:35:28,120 --> 00:35:38,480
Speaker 1: And in both cases, people in this social group openly cheated, and when Scott and I called them out, they got mad and ended the game in both times at the same party.

432
00:35:39,642 --> 00:36:01,920
Speaker 0: Yeah, I don't remember this, but regardless, the point is, if you're at a group, and, you know, let's say you're playing a game with your family and your uncle's cheating, and if you call him out, right, you detected it, you saw it, you maybe even collected evidence, like recorded it on your phone, and such and such, right, you're all on equal footing, the only way that you could actually take action is if the other people at the table give a shit.

433
00:36:02,481 --> 00:36:15,840
Speaker 0: If the other people, if your cousins and your mom and your grandpa, if they don't care, then uncle's just gonna get away with cheating, 'cause there's no power involved or authority to do anything about it.

434
00:36:15,840 --> 00:36:22,178
Speaker 1: I mean, if I cross-check a dude in ice hockey as hard as I can, and the ref doesn't blow the whistle, I totally got away with it.

435
00:36:23,040 --> 00:36:26,380
Speaker 0: Right, and then it would be up to, that's why there's hockey fights, right?

436
00:36:26,961 --> 00:36:29,300
Speaker 0: Hockey fights would be the enforcement method.

437
00:36:29,400 --> 00:36:34,700
Speaker 1: I mean, family games where someone cheats have definitely historically turned into fights.

438
00:36:35,820 --> 00:36:47,200
Speaker 0: Sure enough, right, but the point is, there needs to be some sort of power and authority vested somewhere that you can access to stop the cheating, otherwise nothing else matters.

439
00:36:47,420 --> 00:36:52,880
Speaker 1: Or in the very least, to mete out consequences, however small those consequences might be.

440
00:36:53,140 --> 00:36:57,780
Speaker 0: It could simply be ending the game, not playing with that person anymore.

441
00:36:58,041 --> 00:37:03,159
Speaker 0: You know, correcting the game and fixing the cheat that they did.

442
00:37:03,980 --> 00:37:07,640
Speaker 0: Or, who knows, admonishing them and continuing, right?

443
00:37:08,321 --> 00:37:09,220
Speaker 0: Something, right?

444
00:37:10,083 --> 00:37:14,040
Speaker 0: You can't do any of those things without some amount of power and authority.

445
00:37:14,320 --> 00:37:23,420
Speaker 1: I want to start with a historical example that's also a personal anecdote of cheating in this context, because I feel like examples are a good way to explore anti-cheat.

446
00:37:23,862 --> 00:37:25,128
Speaker 1: So, the game of Euchre.

447
00:37:25,832 --> 00:37:27,540
Speaker 1: We did this in Hearts 2, but we'll just use Euchre.

448
00:37:27,720 --> 00:37:43,420
Speaker 1: It's a simple trick-taking game that people in Michigan play and nobody else has either heard of or cares about, and it's not a great game, I'm not getting into that, but in this game, like in many card games, it is very easy for someone who is skilled at sleight of hand to just cheat.

449
00:37:44,501 --> 00:37:48,880
Speaker 1: Just like, think about an old-timey Western poker game for high-stakes money.

450
00:37:49,583 --> 00:37:53,940
Speaker 1: It is very easy for someone with sleight of hand in poker to cheat in some capacity.

451
00:37:54,660 --> 00:38:02,134
Speaker 0: Yeah, all of these, you know, people who are like, you know, magicians who practice sleight of hand and do all kinds of, like, those people could cheat at cards instead.

452
00:38:02,154 --> 00:38:02,898
Speaker 0: They don't.

453
00:38:03,621 --> 00:38:05,639
Speaker 0: 'Cause magic is a much safer career.

454
00:38:06,921 --> 00:38:11,080
Speaker 0: And if they're good at it, they can make plenty of money doing that, and it's more fun, probably.

455
00:38:11,540 --> 00:38:15,220
Speaker 1: So, how did old-timey Western poker games prevent cheating?

456
00:38:16,660 --> 00:38:28,320
Speaker 1: Yeah, the fact that there really weren't cops or laws for a brief period of time in the Old West, and dipshits with guns would just settle their own problems in horrific ways.

457
00:38:29,342 --> 00:38:34,600
Speaker 0: We didn't pull guns out in Michigan, but-- I'm sure people have been shot for cheating who didn't cheat.

458
00:38:35,000 --> 00:38:35,910
Speaker 1: Oh, yeah, absolutely.

459
00:38:36,152 --> 00:38:40,359
Speaker 0: In fact, I am-- Someone you don't like, just play poker with someone you don't like, accuse them of cheating and shoot 'em.

460
00:38:41,160 --> 00:38:44,060
Speaker 1: Yeah, a perfectly valid strategy if you have no morals.

461
00:38:44,502 --> 00:38:53,340
Speaker 1: So, talking about Euchre, you know, in a modern setting, as a kid, it's very easy to cheat at a game like Euchre, especially because you'd use half a deck of cards.

462
00:38:53,380 --> 00:39:01,700
Speaker 1: Like, it's a small number of cards, you deal five cards to each player, the substance of those cards matter, and you're not allowed to talk to your partner.

463
00:39:01,780 --> 00:39:04,640
Speaker 1: So there's lots of mechanisms one could cheat.

464
00:39:05,280 --> 00:39:12,160
Speaker 1: So the anti-cheat that has been implemented pretty universally in games like Euchre involved things like cutting the deck.

465
00:39:13,082 --> 00:39:14,680
Speaker 1: People don't even do this that much anymore.

466
00:39:14,880 --> 00:39:20,980
Speaker 1: I've found very few people at tables actually want to cut a deck the way most card games used to prescribe.

467
00:39:21,380 --> 00:39:23,180
Speaker 1: It seems like it's a cultural tradition that's disappearing.

468
00:39:23,760 --> 00:39:27,439
Speaker 0: The concept that whoever shuffles-- I always cut the deck when I'm playing real card games.

469
00:39:27,560 --> 00:39:37,280
Speaker 0: Oh, yeah, if I'm playing a dame for real, I always want to offer that, but I've found-- When you played Netrunner, you'd shuffle your deck, hand it to the other person, and then they would shuffle it and hand it back, and then you couldn't shuffle it anymore.

470
00:39:37,983 --> 00:39:41,580
Speaker 1: So if I was the dealer, I shuffle the deck, and I may have cheated.

471
00:39:41,840 --> 00:39:45,740
Speaker 1: I may have lined up the cards in any particular matter I want, because it's really fucking easy to do that.

472
00:39:46,140 --> 00:40:06,839
Speaker 1: But then I had to hand the deck to another player who is opposed to me, and they can't fuss with the deck, but they pick a number of arbitrary cards from the top and set them aside, and I have to take the bottom of that and stick it on top and then deal out the cards, thus ensuring that I don't have full agency over the order of those cards in case I happen to have sleight of hand.

473
00:40:07,500 --> 00:40:20,820
Speaker 1: Now, of course, I was still able to cheat because there are mechanisms like the false cut or dealing from the bottom that are very hard to detect, and those are really only detectable by everyone is watching the dealer.

474
00:40:21,280 --> 00:40:31,680
Speaker 1: That's actually why Euchre requires you to deal 2-3, 2-3, 2-3, because the ritual of that makes it harder to slip a card out from the bottom without someone noticing because so few cards are--.

475
00:40:31,700 --> 00:40:33,130
Speaker 0: Well, on the twos, it's really easy.

476
00:40:33,150 --> 00:40:34,660
Speaker 0: You just take one for the top and one for the bottom.

477
00:40:35,021 --> 00:40:37,068
Speaker 1: Well, usually you go one, two.

478
00:40:37,490 --> 00:40:50,099
Speaker 1: The top and bottom simultaneously, I do that in spotted, but if anyone ever did one motion to deal out two cards, I immediately called bullshit and demanded a redeal, as any savvy player would do.

479
00:40:50,940 --> 00:40:59,040
Speaker 1: But we had this physical mechanism to prevent the most common form of cheating, and it was pretty effective, but again, it required everything Scott said.

480
00:40:59,320 --> 00:41:09,460
Speaker 1: The people at the table had to agree to that and follow it, but the game encoded the rules, the culture encoded the rules, and it was an effective anti-cheat against all but the most extreme magician.

481
00:41:11,063 --> 00:41:21,320
Speaker 0: So this type of cheating, right, because there's different-- I think there's really not that actually many categories of cheating, but this category of cheating is basically not following game procedures, right?

482
00:41:21,480 --> 00:41:35,040
Speaker 0: Games have procedures that you're supposed to follow that are-- have, you know, based on how well the rules are written, have different degrees of explicitness and ambiguity, and if those procedures aren't followed properly, then that's cheating, right?

483
00:41:35,100 --> 00:41:38,880
Speaker 1: Yep, and the rules of a card game, the deck must be shuffled and delivered randomly.

484
00:41:39,920 --> 00:41:45,920
Speaker 0: Yep, and so the procedures themselves are often anti-cheat mechanisms, right?

485
00:41:46,000 --> 00:41:55,580
Speaker 0: It's like if the procedures are followed, and that actually prevents other problems in the game, you know, it makes it harder to cheat in other ways as long as we do these things.

486
00:41:55,820 --> 00:41:57,040
Speaker 1: Case in point, American football.

487
00:41:57,040 --> 00:42:00,759
Speaker 0: It's an anti-cheat mechanism, but also not following them is a form of cheating itself.

488
00:42:01,301 --> 00:42:15,440
Speaker 1: The fact that in American football, the coin flip, the coin is flipped into the air, but it is not caught, it is left to land on the ground, because a flipped and caught coin is very easy to cheat with, and I actually learned how to do this--.

489
00:42:15,440 --> 00:42:25,658
Speaker 0: Also, they use those weird commemorative coins that I believe are 50/50, so-- Never ever-- If you got-- you could get info about the coin in advance, you could predict which side is more likely to land up and--

490
00:42:26,481 --> 00:42:40,040
Speaker 1: But never ever trust, for anything serious, a coin flip where the person flipping catches it, because I learned in RIT, and I can do it pretty reliably, you can make a flipped coin that you catch be whatever face you want.

491
00:42:41,403 --> 00:42:43,859
Speaker 1: Because they spin at a rate that you can predict.

492
00:42:47,741 --> 00:42:56,200
Speaker 0: Anyway, so the way to prevent this kind of cheating, right, is to simply have people pay the fuck attention, right?

493
00:42:56,820 --> 00:42:59,320
Speaker 0: That's all you gotta do to prevent this kind of cheating, right?

494
00:42:59,600 --> 00:43:13,560
Speaker 0: Obviously you need the authority and whatnot, but it's like, if you just pay attention to the procedures, and actually look closely, and follow them, and don't drift off or whatever, it's like, then cheating will be prevented.

495
00:43:13,780 --> 00:43:15,279
Speaker 0: It's really not complicated, right?

496
00:43:15,480 --> 00:43:23,279
Speaker 1: It's like-- No one got away with cheating except the pros at our Euchre tables, because everyone was watching the dealer with an eagle eye.

497
00:43:26,684 --> 00:43:41,340
Speaker 0: As long as things are done openly, where everyone can see them, and people are paying attention because they care, then it would be pretty difficult for somebody to execute the procedures of the game in a way that's shady, right?

498
00:43:41,420 --> 00:43:45,395
Speaker 0: It's like, someone tries to start a game monopoly and deal themselves an extra $500 bill, right?

499
00:43:47,282 --> 00:43:53,400
Speaker 0: It's like, if you just look at everyone's money, you'd be like, "Hey, why you got an extra $500 more than everyone else?

500
00:43:53,460 --> 00:43:54,489
Speaker 0: You're supposed to start with $1100, not

501
00:43:54,489 --> 00:43:55,760
Speaker 0: $1600.".

502
00:43:55,760 --> 00:43:55,848
Speaker 0: Yeah.

503
00:43:56,780 --> 00:43:56,868
Speaker 0: Yeah.

504
00:43:57,521 --> 00:43:59,359
Speaker 0: It's like, it's pretty much that simple.

505
00:43:59,921 --> 00:44:01,960
Speaker 0: It's like, just fucking count.

506
00:44:03,420 --> 00:44:11,640
Speaker 1: But then there's the really thorny space that especially applies to video games of, how did you frame it, modifying the equipment in some way.

507
00:44:11,701 --> 00:44:13,880
Speaker 0: Right, so this is the hardest kind of cheating.

508
00:44:14,020 --> 00:44:23,440
Speaker 0: It's like, games, especially sports, and also esports included, are games that involve specialized equipment, right?

509
00:44:23,720 --> 00:44:29,260
Speaker 0: I think you'd also, even if you think about anything that's a sport, whether it's baseball, corked bats, right?

510
00:44:30,761 --> 00:44:33,800
Speaker 0: Running, which is drugged up human bodies, right?

511
00:44:34,100 --> 00:44:36,100
Speaker 0: The equipment is your body, which has been modified.

512
00:44:36,680 --> 00:44:40,758
Speaker 0: Jenga, someone shaves some of the Jenga tiles like this, logs, right?

513
00:44:41,744 --> 00:44:46,259
Speaker 1: Ice hockey, someone's got to stick with a little too much curve or a goalie pad that's a little too big.

514
00:44:46,841 --> 00:44:50,120
Speaker 0: Playing cards, someone marks them or clips them, right?

515
00:44:50,620 --> 00:44:54,620
Speaker 1: Counter-Strike, you've got an aimbot running on your computer next to Counter-Strike.

516
00:44:54,620 --> 00:44:57,000
Speaker 0: Right, the software is part of the equipment, right?

517
00:44:58,263 --> 00:45:00,498
Speaker 0: You've got a robot that moves the mouse for you.

518
00:45:02,481 --> 00:45:11,140
Speaker 0: There's equipment and materials involved in games, and if those materials aren't according to a particular spec, then that's cheating.

519
00:45:11,943 --> 00:45:22,940
Speaker 0: The worst case would be something like Formula One, where it's like the car must comply with these exact measurements, weights, et cetera, materials, you know, costs and shapes and all kinds of rules.

520
00:45:23,762 --> 00:45:30,515
Speaker 0: And the way you stop this kind of cheating is also pretty simple, which is inspect the equipment closely, right?

521
00:45:32,380 --> 00:45:40,720
Speaker 0: The difficulty, of course, is that sometimes you can't really inspect the equipment or be a super-certain, and this is where you get the performance-enhancing drugs, right?

522
00:45:40,780 --> 00:45:43,796
Speaker 0: It's like you can't always be 100% sure.

523
00:45:44,620 --> 00:45:50,377
Speaker 0: We have tests, we drug-tested someone, they got caught, and then they say, "Oh, no, my...".

524
00:45:51,021 --> 00:45:53,838
Speaker 0: The reason a Russian figure skater, her excuse was that...

525
00:45:54,100 --> 00:45:55,220
Speaker 1: What, the strawberry or something?

526
00:45:55,660 --> 00:46:13,500
Speaker 0: Yeah, so the excuse, if you haven't heard the excuse of the Russian figure skater, is that her grandpa would make this strawberry dessert, like family meal thing, and grandpa smashed his pills on a cutting board and then made the strawberry dessert on the cutting board later.

527
00:46:14,300 --> 00:46:19,520
Speaker 0: And so somehow some minuscule amount of whatever got into the strawberry.

528
00:46:19,780 --> 00:46:23,920
Speaker 1: I was walking down the street and I just fell on a needle filled with steroids.

529
00:46:24,500 --> 00:46:29,638
Speaker 0: And that was somehow enough for the drug test to come up positive, whatever minuscule amount that might have been.

530
00:46:31,425 --> 00:46:33,060
Speaker 0: It's like, yeah, obviously that's bullshit.

531
00:46:35,286 --> 00:46:39,440
Speaker 0: But even at the same time, something like that could happen.

532
00:46:40,523 --> 00:46:57,144
Speaker 0: If I was in the Olympic Village and I know that I'm the second-fastest runner and I'm chilling out with the first-fastest runner and he leaves his smoothie on the table and I sprinkle some drug, some performance-enhancing drug powder in his smoothie, I'm chilling

533
00:46:57,305 --> 00:47:05,420
Speaker 1: out in the Overwatch Village before a tournament and I see that one of my opponents has left their computer unlocked.

534
00:47:05,500 --> 00:47:08,598
Speaker 1: I go install some cheating software on it.

535
00:47:09,400 --> 00:47:10,247
Speaker 0: Right now that is cheating.

536
00:47:10,650 --> 00:47:11,860
Speaker 0: You're the one who's cheating, not them.

537
00:47:12,384 --> 00:47:13,600
Speaker 0: And they're the one who gets caught for cheating.

538
00:47:13,640 --> 00:47:16,960
Speaker 1: I mean, if you spike someone else's drink with steroids, you're cheating.

539
00:47:17,502 --> 00:47:18,420
Speaker 0: Well, that's also a crime.

540
00:47:19,871 --> 00:47:32,900
Speaker 1: But this comes down to the onerousness of imposing these checks and rules coupled with the possibility of consequences and coupled with the stakes involved.

541
00:47:33,300 --> 00:47:50,200
Speaker 1: So Counter-Strike or a video game like Helldiver, all these video game situations, anti-cheat software has to walk a fine line between preventing someone from using software they shouldn't to get an unfair advantage in the game and breaking your computer or spying on you or basically itself being malware.

542
00:47:51,280 --> 00:47:55,780
Speaker 0: Right, you have to look at how serious is the game that we're playing, right?

543
00:47:55,920 --> 00:47:57,086
Speaker 0: Is this the World Series?

544
00:47:57,127 --> 00:48:01,160
Speaker 0: Yeah, we're fucking x-raying every goddamn baseball bat that enters this building, right?

545
00:48:02,142 --> 00:48:05,860
Speaker 1: I'd let him use the cork bats, though, because the cork bats every study shows aren't better.

546
00:48:06,242 --> 00:48:07,339
Speaker 1: In fact, they're probably worse.

547
00:48:08,081 --> 00:48:11,020
Speaker 0: I'm just saying it's against the rules whether it's better or not, right?

548
00:48:11,080 --> 00:48:14,920
Speaker 0: So therefore, you're cheating if you try it, regardless of whether it's actually going to help you.

549
00:48:15,185 --> 00:48:15,879
Speaker 0: It's against the rules.

550
00:48:16,461 --> 00:48:17,199
Speaker 0: You know, it's the Olympics.

551
00:48:17,422 --> 00:48:22,380
Speaker 0: Yeah, everyone's going to live in this village where everything is closely guarded and you can't sneak anything in, hopefully, right?

552
00:48:22,461 --> 00:48:23,296
Speaker 0: Even though you probably can't.

553
00:48:24,402 --> 00:48:26,760
Speaker 0: Is it Poker Night at RIM's house?

554
00:48:27,040 --> 00:48:27,698
Speaker 0: Yeah, guess what?

555
00:48:28,503 --> 00:48:30,359
Speaker 0: If you use marked cards, you'll probably get away with it.

556
00:48:31,021 --> 00:48:33,240
Speaker 0: Because nobody's looking that close.

557
00:48:33,640 --> 00:48:34,826
Speaker 1: The $12 you make is not worth anyone's time.

558
00:48:38,304 --> 00:48:39,240
Speaker 0: Is it a poker tournament?

559
00:48:39,320 --> 00:48:49,979
Speaker 0: Yeah, those decks are all coming from a vault where they've been all checked and printed especially, and no one's ever seen them before, and we're going to change the deck every game.

560
00:48:50,761 --> 00:48:53,520
Speaker 1: In that case, cheating usually would come down to corruption.

561
00:48:55,027 --> 00:48:55,380
Speaker 0: Indeed.

562
00:48:55,840 --> 00:49:00,759
Speaker 1: But corruption is really just an evolved form of violating the procedures of the game.

563
00:49:01,921 --> 00:49:07,800
Speaker 1: Like, the referee is supposed to call X and you bribe them to not notice X at a very particular moment.

564
00:49:08,362 --> 00:49:20,960
Speaker 0: The difference between corruption and simply procedures not being followed properly is that in corruption, this is where the people who have the power and authority to stop the cheating are in fact part of the cheating.

565
00:49:21,200 --> 00:49:22,280
Speaker 0: And that's what's different, right?

566
00:49:22,721 --> 00:49:29,880
Speaker 0: If the hockey ref wants your team to win and he just calls them bullshit penalties a couple times, right?

567
00:49:30,682 --> 00:49:35,860
Speaker 0: It's like, you know, yes, they're not following the correct procedure.

568
00:49:35,940 --> 00:49:39,200
Speaker 0: They called a penalty inappropriately when no penalty was warranted.

569
00:49:39,801 --> 00:49:47,120
Speaker 0: So that is true, but the authority to do anything about it is vested in the cheating party.

570
00:49:47,444 --> 00:49:47,516
Speaker 0: Yup.

571
00:49:48,181 --> 00:49:49,659
Speaker 0: So what the fuck are you going to do about that?

572
00:49:49,840 --> 00:49:53,159
Speaker 1: That's why the Black Sox are known as the Black Sox to this day.

573
00:49:54,020 --> 00:49:54,377
Speaker 0: That's right.

574
00:49:55,341 --> 00:49:57,393
Speaker 0: But that was actually, the refs weren't in on that.

575
00:49:58,620 --> 00:49:59,480
Speaker 0: The umps weren't in on that.

576
00:49:59,520 --> 00:50:03,299
Speaker 0: That was the players were losing on purpose and betting on themselves to lose.

577
00:50:04,525 --> 00:50:11,499
Speaker 1: Well then it's the question of, are you cheating at game A or is game A's cheating just a part of a larger cheating of game B?

578
00:50:11,780 --> 00:50:13,339
Speaker 1: Game B being society in some way.

579
00:50:14,740 --> 00:50:27,119
Speaker 1: But for what we just said before, the analogy of corruption in a video game would be what if the anti-cheat in Overwatch had a "if rim is playing, do not enforce" because rim is part of the fix.

580
00:50:28,420 --> 00:50:31,720
Speaker 0: Yeah, rim works at the game company and he puts in code to help him.

581
00:50:31,861 --> 00:50:33,190
Speaker 0: This has happened before.

582
00:50:33,371 --> 00:50:34,580
Speaker 0: I can't remember the exact instance.

583
00:50:35,481 --> 00:50:44,480
Speaker 0: But it has happened before where people who worked at game companies gave themselves boosts in the games for their own favor.

584
00:50:45,140 --> 00:50:53,500
Speaker 1: Now one interesting sort of side specific to video games is I think there's the situation where the game has high stakes and matters.

585
00:50:53,760 --> 00:50:59,060
Speaker 1: Like the professional championship of a game where that requires this high degree of anti-cheat.

586
00:50:59,702 --> 00:51:06,160
Speaker 1: But there's also a separate factor of a game that people can just play, the masses can play, like Counter-Strike.

587
00:51:07,241 --> 00:51:28,903
Speaker 1: In the era when Counter-Strike became popular, but before there was effective anti-cheat mechanisms the presence of cheating, it wasn't high stakes, the stakes of Counter-Strike are basically negative just playing it against randos, but it literally ruined the game and made it unplayable at all if anyone cheated.

588
00:51:30,187 --> 00:51:34,820
Speaker 1: In which case, anti-cheat is required for the game to exist in the first place.

589
00:51:35,600 --> 00:51:42,479
Speaker 1: And because people wanted to play Counter-Strike, that's kind of why people were willing to put up with Punkbuster and VAC in those early days.

590
00:51:43,440 --> 00:51:44,042
Speaker 0: That's correct.

591
00:51:45,749 --> 00:51:53,580
Speaker 0: So the last category of cheating that I can think of at least is basically there's an illegal game state that is created.

592
00:51:53,660 --> 00:52:04,037
Speaker 0: Now you can sort of say that maybe procedures weren't followed correctly, but it could just be like "Hey, somebody did something they're not allowed to

593
00:52:04,077 --> 00:52:04,218
Speaker 0: do.".

594
00:52:05,663 --> 00:52:21,138
Speaker 0: Maybe you're playing a collectible card game and there's a card that says "You can only have two greens" and someone puts out three greens and forgets that there's a rule for two greens and no one catches it.

595
00:52:22,162 --> 00:52:24,170
Speaker 0: And it's like, they might have done that on purpose.

596
00:52:24,913 --> 00:52:26,460
Speaker 0: Just slipped a third green out there.

597
00:52:27,581 --> 00:52:32,440
Speaker 0: I guess that technically, it's a matter of semantics, was that not following a procedure correctly.

598
00:52:33,061 --> 00:52:40,820
Speaker 0: But it's sort of different from not following a procedure correctly in that it's not just like you didn't shuffle the cards or you didn't cut them.

599
00:52:40,920 --> 00:52:46,158
Speaker 0: It's like the game is actually in a state in which the rules are broken.

600
00:52:48,860 --> 00:52:52,499
Speaker 1: Two pucks appear on the ice and we're not sure which one was the official puck.

601
00:52:53,440 --> 00:52:53,541
Speaker 0: Right.

602
00:52:53,561 --> 00:52:56,117
Speaker 0: They have a rule, which is you blow the whistle, you stop play, etc.

603
00:52:57,040 --> 00:53:00,000
Speaker 0: of what to do in that situation so it doesn't completely break the game.

604
00:53:00,581 --> 00:53:04,420
Speaker 0: But it's like you still had, for a time, an illegal game state.

605
00:53:04,480 --> 00:53:09,520
Speaker 1: And this is where you need a mechanism to detect, prevent, and mitigate those situations.

606
00:53:09,982 --> 00:53:24,640
Speaker 1: And I think you need a separate mechanism on at what point do you determine intentionality and/or apply consequences beyond the consequences of making the game into a good state or ending the game because it's permanently ruined.

607
00:53:26,220 --> 00:53:28,739
Speaker 1: Hockey, like a good example, just to keep bringing hockey analogies.

608
00:53:30,101 --> 00:53:36,740
Speaker 1: Delay of game, if you're in your zone and you shoot the puck just out of the arena, like off into the crowd, that's a delay of game penalty.

609
00:53:37,181 --> 00:53:39,200
Speaker 1: It doesn't matter if you did it on purpose or not.

610
00:53:39,300 --> 00:53:51,315
Speaker 1: The fact that it happened is a penalty that interacts with the game because otherwise people would very clearly, "Oops, I accidentally knocked it out of play in spicy

611
00:53:51,355 --> 00:53:52,018
Speaker 1: situations.".

612
00:53:52,500 --> 00:53:55,356
Speaker 1: "Oops, I accidentally had four Sol Rings in my magic

613
00:53:55,397 --> 00:53:55,618
Speaker 1: deck.".

614
00:53:56,060 --> 00:54:01,518
Speaker 1: "I guess my dad left them on the cutting board and I must have accidentally shuffled them in right before the

615
00:54:01,538 --> 00:54:01,919
Speaker 1: tournament.".

616
00:54:02,981 --> 00:54:03,064
Speaker 0: Yep.

617
00:54:05,781 --> 00:54:10,014
Speaker 0: So yeah, to prevent illegal game states, the best thing you can do is to have a video game, right?

618
00:54:12,541 --> 00:54:19,680
Speaker 0: Or some other sort of system that monitors the game state and that system is not corrupted.

619
00:54:20,821 --> 00:54:26,940
Speaker 0: And it monitors the game state and notifies or does something when the game state is no good, right?

620
00:54:27,040 --> 00:54:36,216
Speaker 0: So if you have a video game and there's a PVP video game and there's a server and the server obviously doesn't have cheat installed on it because it's at the game company's data

621
00:54:36,236 --> 00:54:36,437
Speaker 1: center,

622
00:54:38,981 --> 00:54:51,380
Speaker 0: it can detect, hopefully, it might not be able to detect if someone's cheating, depending on the type of cheating, but it can detect like, "Hey, so-and-so has more ammo than that gun can possibly hold," right?

623
00:54:51,861 --> 00:54:55,899
Speaker 0: "Hey, so-and-so has a thousand health in a game where max health is a

624
00:54:55,959 --> 00:54:56,180
Speaker 0: hundred.".

625
00:54:56,260 --> 00:55:00,916
Speaker 1: A really common one in the Quake days was, "So-and-so's character reached a speed that should be

626
00:55:00,956 --> 00:55:01,498
Speaker 1: impossible.".

627
00:55:02,242 --> 00:55:02,805
Speaker 0: Exactly.

628
00:55:03,892 --> 00:55:05,260
Speaker 0: Those sorts of things can be detected.

629
00:55:05,400 --> 00:55:11,800
Speaker 0: In the real world, then all you can do is just, like I said, for the procedures is vigilance, right?

630
00:55:12,140 --> 00:55:21,660
Speaker 0: You have to be paying attention, carefully checking the game state against the legal game state to see if anything bad has happened.

631
00:55:21,880 --> 00:55:42,010
Speaker 0: And depending on the design of the game, there could be situations where if the game is poorly written or poorly designed or has a flaw that no one recognized because even the best games aren't perfect, where you could follow all the procedures correctly and one rule could conflict another rule and following correct procedures puts the game in a state.

632
00:55:42,311 --> 00:55:45,220
Speaker 0: that breaks another rule about what's a legal state.

633
00:55:47,101 --> 00:56:06,700
Speaker 1: God, now I think back to, I don't know if it was a bad state or we just were unwilling to interrogate the rules to a conclusion, but I remember the one time since college that we played Diplomacy and we ended the game partly because a series of conflicts formed a perfect loop around the game board.

634
00:56:06,920 --> 00:56:07,912
Speaker 0: I think there's an answer for that.

635
00:56:07,932 --> 00:56:08,498
Speaker 0: we just didn't know.

636
00:56:08,660 --> 00:56:10,079
Speaker 1: I think there is, but we couldn't figure it out.

637
00:56:11,901 --> 00:56:12,204
Speaker 0: No.

638
00:56:12,547 --> 00:56:13,960
Speaker 0: I'm sure the internet has the answer somewhere.

639
00:56:14,961 --> 00:56:16,880
Speaker 0: Diplomacy is a well-trod ground.

640
00:56:17,140 --> 00:56:34,159
Speaker 1: At least in tabletop games, what that really comes down to is a well-designed game provides enough avenues and information about transactions and state changes in the game to ensure that players who are paying attention can notice if something has happened that shouldn't have happened.

641
00:56:34,960 --> 00:56:47,660
Speaker 1: Badly designed games will often leave opportunities where someone will not cheat on purpose, but someone who's not paying attention will just collect way too many victory points or pick up too many cards or have too many cards in their hand and no one notices.

642
00:56:48,541 --> 00:56:53,540
Speaker 1: I think that's just good game design to avoid those kinds of ambiguous possibilities.

643
00:56:55,241 --> 00:57:10,886
Speaker 0: I guess the final thing you can do in your actual life, most people listening are probably not game adjudicators, game designers maybe, is the thing that you could do in your actual life of game and sport playing to prevent cheating.

644
00:57:10,926 --> 00:57:15,840
Speaker 0: the most is to simply refuse to play with a cheating person.

645
00:57:17,141 --> 00:57:28,080
Speaker 0: If cheating people, if peoples don't play games with them, then that helps quelch the overall cheating that goes on in the world.

646
00:57:28,882 --> 00:57:37,780
Speaker 1: This is why I'm totally okay with online ortho games, the aggressive perma-banning of people who cheat, I am 100% there for that, I wish they could be more aggressive.

647
00:57:38,600 --> 00:57:47,639
Speaker 0: Yeah, it's like if your uncle is cheating and you don't have the authority and you can't stop him and no one cares, just stop playing, you don't have to play.

648
00:57:48,060 --> 00:57:54,940
Speaker 1: And every Christmas when the family asks you to play Monopoly with the cheating uncle, just say no and remind everyone that he's a piece of shit.

649
00:57:55,523 --> 00:57:56,066
Speaker 0: Yeah, just like.

650
00:57:56,147 --> 00:58:00,952
Speaker 0: no, you guys cheat, I don't want to play with the cheating way, and you guys don't seem to care about the cheating.

651
00:58:00,972 --> 00:58:05,420
Speaker 0: so you go play your cheating game and I'll stay over here with the pet and the dog, I don't know, right?

652
